  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSplit flow aloft is expected across Kansas today and Friday. The\nnet result is a dry forecast and warm (above normal) highs and\nmild lows. Winds will remain on the light side today as the High\nPlains lee trough remains on the weak side. Eventually, by the\nweekend, broad southwesterly flow aloft is expected across the\nstate. This will result in the deepening of the lee trough with\nbreezy and gusty winds expected Friday through Monday. Even with\nthe winds and warmth, significant fire weather concerns are not\nexpected due to recent rainfall and unresponsive fuels. For\ntemperatures, highs and lows are expected to be above normal.\nNormal highs for this time of year are in the mid 70s with lows\nin the mid to upper 40s. Highs Friday are expected to peak in \nthe upper 80s to around 90F. Lows will be in the mix of 50s to \n60s. Similar highs and lows are expected over the weekend with \nhighs in the upper 80s to around 90F and lows in the 50s to 60s,\nwith more 60s by the end of the weekend.\n\nAttention then turns to the middle portion of next week. Will\nremnant tropical moisture left from what would be Simon\ninfluence our area?! Right now, there is considerable spread in\nthe heavier rainfall axis from both the GEFS and ENS. The latest\nNBM has chance pops next Wednesday. The latest NHC forecast\ntrack from Simon does have landfall farther south than what we\nsaw from Polo. So, it is possible this system could track\nfarther south taking much of the moisture with it. Again, since\nthere is considerable spread in the ensembles, it is hard to get\nexcited or hopeful at this point. The bottom line is we will\nneed to look for ensemble trends in the exact storm track before\nwe begin to discuss particular rainfall amounts.",
